  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nWelcome to meteorological fall! It will not feel much like fall\nthough with temperatures continuing to be warm (generally in the\n80s) even for the peak of summer. In addition we also have a \nchance for some strong to severe thunderstorms tonight and\nalmost daily chances for more rain/storms. Even though next \nweek is starting to look a little cooler, it will be a while \nbefore we get fall weather.\n\nToday and Tonight... Another morning of ample radiative cooling\nand light winds is providing for some patchy fog. Some areas \ncould be locally dense, but it will not be widespread. The cold \nfront has now passed through all of the area, but it won't be \nmuch cooler today as the warm airmass remains over us. The \nbigger difference with this frontal passage is a drop in dew \npoints. It will not be dry by any means, but not as high as what\nwe saw in the past two days. We could still see some dew points \napproach 70 along the I-90 corridor as some of the moisture \nacross Iowa advects north. The main impact for today will come \novernight as a shortwave moves over the Upper Midwest. \nConvection is expected to initiate to our west across South \nDakota associated with this synoptic feature. As this \nconvection heads east upscale growth into a MCS appears like \nthe most likely solution. MCS maintenance looks favorable as it \nwould be fed by the LLJ. The biggest question right now is how \nfar north will this be? Most of the CAM guidance favors the I-90\ncorridor, but some outliers get as far north as the southern \nTwin Cities metro area. Conceptually though the southern \nsolution where most of the CAMs are make the most sense as this \nwould be where the best environment would be. MU CAPE still \nlooks close to 3000 J/kg and deep layer shear also looks strong.\nDue to the anticipated storm mode damaging winds would be the \nmain risk for tonight. \n\nWednesday into the Weekend... The summer pattern will continue\ninto the holiday weekend. Wednesday looks to be a little cooler\nwith highs more in the lower 80s as we look to get a break from\nthe WAA. This won't last long as the WAA comes back Wednesday\nnight into Thursday to both bring our next rain chance and\nwarmer temperatures. There will be some lift with the warm front\nlifting north which should give us enough for another rain\nchance. As we throughout this period have plenty of warm and\nmoist air to fill the other \"ingredients\" needed for rainfall.\nSo where we can get enough forcing there should be rain. Once\nagain the best chance for rain this period (Wednesday night) is\nacross southern Minnesota. This will also provide for a warming\ntrend with highs on Thursday in the upper 80s. This heat will\nhold through Saturday before some cooler air advects back in\nearly next week. Thursday night looks like a similar setup with\na similar impact, just a little more spread in ensemble guidance.\nWhat is less certain is who will see rain and storms as these \nmore mesoscale features are not apparent this far out and our\nmore synoptic models are too broad with QPF. Friday night into \nthe weekend starts to see much less clustering in the global \nensembles and therefore less confidence in the rain forecast. \nMembership shows continued rain chances, but widely scattered \nfrom member to member. So if you have Labor Day Weekend plans \nkeep an eye on the weather forecast as we get closer and start \nto get a better idea of when thunderstorms could occur. \n\nNext week... This uncertain pattern remains into early next\nweek. NBM therefore keeps low PoPs spread across every day. As\nthis gets into days 1 to 3 details will come into alignment. \nWhat is more certain is that there does look to be some cooling.\nStill much above normal temperatures, but instead of 10+ above \nnormal it looks closer to 5 above normal. Normals right now are \nin the upper 70s and will soon fall into the mid 70s.",
